being close to the terminally ill needs  a new layer of care during the  coronavirus crisis we filmed Pat cowling  from outside her hospice window keeping  a safe distance for the nurses and  assistants here they must wear gloves  aprons and masks but a national shortage  means it's a scramble each week to  source the gear supplies are due to run  out within days unless more is delivered  we're having to contact people to beg  steal and borrow from different  different agencies and different  organizations who were all always  willing to help us but it always seems  that it's only when you get to that  critical crisis point that suddenly some  PPE will arrive it's a plea heard across  the health sector from frontline  hospitals to hospices under pressure  ministers said a shipment of supplies  would arrive today it is the case that  there's a consignment of personal  protection equipment that's arriving  from Turkey which contains tens of  thousands of gowns but barely two hours  later it emerged the just-in-time  delivery had been delayed not good  enough say medics this is devastating  news I'm so sorry for our doctors and  their patients and I would like to ask  the government the words of the minister  himself Robert Jen Rick what should we  do shipments have been coming in from  overseas including this plane load from  China but there's global competition and  limited supply now the government has  charged the man who oversaw the London  Olympics with boosting the production of  protective equipment at home we do  recognise that we need to be working  constantly in order to be able to boost  supplies this is why we brought in law  Dayton we don't want to see anyone in a  setting that doesn't have the equipment  that they need to do their job but  that's exactly the risk the nurses at  this hospice face if their stocks run  out their patients really don't have  time for any delay Deborah Haynes Sky  News
 

